WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.445
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.845 --> 00:00:21.220
HOCKFIELD: It's great to see
you all here this afternoon.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.330
For those of you
who don't know, I'm

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:24.177
Susan Hockfield, the
president of MIT.

00:00:24.177 --> 00:00:26.260
And I'd like to welcome
you all to this incredibly

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.490
special event.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:33.250
We are enormously grateful
to our alumnus Mr. Mohammed

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:36.520
Jameel for making it possible
for Dr. Muhammad Yunus

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.000
to visit MIT today.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.750
He's been visiting
with the Poverty Action

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:42.670
Lab and the Department
of Economics.

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:45.010
And he's giving us this
wonderful presentation

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:47.230
on ending global poverty.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:50.860
It's a great honor to have
Dr. Yunus on our campus.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:54.760
Dr. Yunus is internationally
recognized for his work

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:58.900
in alleviating power poverty
and empowering poor women.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.870
He's the founder of
the Grameen Bank,

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.070
a microcredit
institution, created

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:06.040
to provide the poor with
tiny amounts of working

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.650
capital for self-employment.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:12.160
Since its launch three decades
ago as an action research

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:16.820
project, the bank has
made more than $5 billion

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:20.950
in collateral-free loans
to 5 million borrowers

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:22.460
in Bangladesh.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.450
96% of the borrowers are women.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:28.240
And the bank now lends
half a billion a year

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.240
to the poorest of the poor,
while maintaining a repayment

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.570
rate of 99%.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.790
This innovative approach
to alleviating poverty

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:39.970
has inspired a global
microcredit movement

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:42.760
that reaches out to millions
of poor women on all five

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:44.960
continents.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.120
Through this work,
Dr. Yunus may well

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.990
be the most influential
economist in the world.

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:54.380
Dr. Yunus has kindly indicated
that he will take questions

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:55.640
after his remarks.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.310
And Dean Philip Khoury from
the School of Humanities, Arts,

00:01:58.310 --> 00:01:59.750
and Social Sciences
will moderate

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.580
that portion of this
afternoon's presentation.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.880
But for now, I
would ask you please

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.200
to join me in welcoming
Dr. Muhammad Yunus to MIT.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:08.700
[INAUDIBLE]

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.140
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.620
YUNUS: Thank you.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.520
Thank you.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:18.500
Thank you.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:22.910
Thank you very much.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:29.720
Thank you very much.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.680
It's a great honor to be here.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:36.950
And thank you, Susan,
for introducing me.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:48.420
And I'm so happy to talk
about the kind of work I do.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:49.710
It will be very brief.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:53.538
I hope I had more
time to elaborate.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:05.400
People tell me, what's so great
about giving such a tiny loan

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.135
to poor people?

00:03:10.993 --> 00:03:13.160
So I don't know how to
answer that question-- what's

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:18.970
so great about giving so
tiny loans to poor people?

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:22.600
So one idea that came to my
mind, maybe, I thought people

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:29.430
understand it better if
I put it this way that

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:35.810
imagine a situation
where suddenly,

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:40.060
all the banks in the
world got closed,

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:43.847
shut down, non-functional.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:49.470
What will happen to
people around the world?

00:03:53.182 --> 00:03:55.540
And then thinking
about it, I thought

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.150
this would be a good
Hollywood story.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:01.950
You can build a
good film out of it.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:08.860
It would be a horror movie,
like Godzilla or something.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:23.680
About 2/3 of the world
population situation

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.940
is like that.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:30.025
For them, financial
institutions do not exist.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:40.450
But the horror that it should
have created in their life,

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:47.110
neither they see it
nor we, the people who

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:49.510
have banks around us, see it.

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:52.610
Because we took it as
a part of our life.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:02.800
But that's what it is for
2/3 of the world population.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:06.330
Doors of the
financial institutions

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:07.400
have closed to them.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:15.690
So their life has to be
designed another way.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:22.590
And when you say other way,
it's not a very pleasant way.

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:29.140
Because we created a world
which goes around with money.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:31.750
You need money at
every step of the way.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:37.890
If you don't have
the first dollar,

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:39.840
you can't catch
the next dollars.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.570
You need $1 to catch $1.

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:46.068
And nobody gives
them the first dollar

00:05:46.068 --> 00:05:47.110
to catch the next dollar.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:52.750
And that's the situation,
what you describe as poverty.

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:59.290
It's not their fault
that they are poor.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:03.990
We simply refuse
them the privilege

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:06.820
of getting out of poverty.

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:09.150
So when we talk about
eliminating poverty

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:16.730
in the world, we are not
thinking about some people who

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:25.670
don't know what to do in their
life, illiterate, ignorant,

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:29.020
and that's why they are poor.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:32.330
That's not the way it is.

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:35.310
They're as good human
being as anybody else.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:38.020
As enterprising as anybody else.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:41.540
As smart as anybody else.

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:46.090
But simply, they are denied
of certain basic amenities

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:49.360
to use their talent,
use their creativity,

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:52.030
use their ingenuity to
move out of poverty.

00:06:55.190 --> 00:07:00.400
So back in the '70s
when I was teaching

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.550
in one of the universities
in Bangladesh,

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:05.760
Bangladesh was going
through a terrible famine.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:18.970
Famine is a horrible situation
where people die of hunger,

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:21.820
just not being able to eat.

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:24.560
There's not food.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:27.290
So it's not a very pleasant
thing in a situation like that,

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:31.520
teaching elegant
theories of economics.

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:35.210
Writing down all your
equations and so on.

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:39.450
And telling students
how to overcome

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:44.970
all the economic difficulties,
empowered by the theories

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:48.320
that we have in our textbooks.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:53.330
That empowerment doesn't get
translated into real world

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:55.190
just outside the
classroom, where

00:07:55.190 --> 00:08:01.590
people are going through
this experience of famine.

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:06.450
So that trip took me out of the
classroom and trying to see,

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:10.320
is there something
that can be done?

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:13.490
And out of those
tiny little things,

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:17.390
one thing that I
confronted [? or ?] I

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:21.370
noticed in everyday
way in that how people

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:23.500
suffered for tiny little money.

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:29.550
And then, I took
a student of mine,

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:34.409
went around in the village
to list people who had

00:08:34.409 --> 00:08:36.404
to borrow from the moneylender.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:42.280
And when our list
was completed, there

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:45.800
were 42 names on that
list who took money

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:48.730
from the moneylenders.

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:52.970
And the total amount for the
whole 42 of them was $27.

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:59.830
So again, imagine
professor teaching

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:02.740
in the classroom about
the five-year development

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:05.200
plan for the country.

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:07.770
All the millions of dollars
and billions of dollars

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:14.250
you need to invest to overcome
the poverty in the country.

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:21.360
Has no idea that few meters
from that classroom, people

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.535
are not waiting for those
millions and billions.

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:31.500
Their life is going through
a miserable condition

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:37.580
because of few
pennies, less than $1.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:43.620
So my first response was to
give $27 from my own pocket,

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:50.090
and tell them, please return
the money to the moneylenders.

00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:54.710
And work whatever you want
to work with this money,

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:57.530
and be free.

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:02.930
And that sense of liberation
that came to them hooked me on.

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:11.250
I asked myself that if you can
make so many people so happy,

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:17.716
such a small amount of money,
shouldn't you do more of it?

00:10:17.716 --> 00:10:21.930
So I was thinking
how to do more of it.

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:24.930
I could give more
money from my pocket,

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:28.040
but I thought that only
a solution, ultimately.

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:29.660
I was thinking more
something which

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:33.870
will be generating a system.

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:36.110
So I thought I
should link them up

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:38.915
with the bank which is
located in the campus.

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:43.370
Which I tried.

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:45.890
When I proposed to
the bank manager,

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:49.070
he literally fell from the sky.

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:51.710
He couldn't believe
that I'm saying

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:55.990
that he has to lend to these
people, this tiny amount.

00:10:55.990 --> 00:11:00.250
He argued, very forcibly, that
the bank cannot lend money

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:03.310
to the poor people.

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:06.140
Poor people are not bankable.

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:09.150
Poor people are
not creditworthy.

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:13.580
He made all kinds of arguments,
which didn't make sense to me.

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:16.620
So I made out my arguments.

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:19.900
It's a no-go
situation, either side.

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:24.680
So anyway, the long story
continued for several months,

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:29.690
meeting all the bank
officials, trying to persuade

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:32.420
to take loans for the people.

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:34.190
Nobody would agree.

00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:39.640
Ultimately, it was solved by
offering myself as a guarantor.

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:42.010
I said I'll sign
all your papers,

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:44.870
since your rules require it.

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:48.250
And you cannot accept them
because they don't have

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:49.880
the collateral.

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:53.530
And the banking system is
built around the collateral.

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:57.510
So the only way I can do it is
to offer myself as a guarantor.

00:11:57.510 --> 00:11:59.490
That still took another
couple of months

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:03.030
of correspondence, and
discussions, and persuasions.

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:05.850
And finally, it was resolved.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:10.601
And I started giving loans
to the people in the village.

00:12:10.601 --> 00:12:14.560
The bank manager said you can
say goodbye to your money.

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:16.860
This is not going to come back.

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:19.320
I said, I'll take a chance.

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:21.180
I took my chance.

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:24.450
And along with it, I tried
to make some simple rules

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:27.030
to encourage them to pay back.

00:12:27.030 --> 00:12:30.150
And it worked.

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:33.080
I got very excited that
it worked because the bank

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:34.310
manager said it won't work.

00:12:38.870 --> 00:12:42.380
When I came to the bank
manager to give the good news

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:45.680
that everything is complete,
all the money came back,

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:50.100
he remained totally unimpressed.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:53.990
He said maybe you did it a
few people in one village.

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:57.110
A professor can do all kinds
of miracles in one village.

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:02.383
We don't take it seriously.

00:13:02.383 --> 00:13:03.550
So I said, what should I do?

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:06.000
He said you should do
it in two villages.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.770
I said, OK, I'll do
it in two villages.

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:09.540
I did it in two villages.

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:12.090
It worked beautifully.

00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:17.170
But he remains as
unimpressed as he was.

00:13:17.170 --> 00:13:18.130
So this is the story.

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:20.600
I go on doing it, and he
raises the number of villages.

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:28.810
And after I have done it several
times, then it dawned on me--

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:34.180
if I do it for the whole world,
he will not change his mind.

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:36.550
Because his mind is made.

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:40.220
Banking cannot be
done with poor people.

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:41.770
And I cannot undo that mind.

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:51.430
I said if now he says poor
people are not creditworthy,

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:54.860
they are not bankable,
I will scream.

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:57.470
I'll scream at the
top of my voice.

00:13:57.470 --> 00:14:00.470
Said that's a lie,
that's not true.

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:04.070
Because I have done
it, I've seen it.

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:09.490
I said the real question is not
whether people are creditworthy

00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:10.540
or not.

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:16.400
The real question is what
the banks are peopleworthy.

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:19.390
And they are not.

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:21.900
So I thought maybe
I should now create

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:27.630
a bank, which is different
from the banks that I know.

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:34.200
And I made a proposal
to the central bank,

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:36.940
that please allow me to
set up this bank which

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:39.590
will work for the poor people.

00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:43.010
Central bank says we never
heard of that kind of bank.

00:14:43.010 --> 00:14:45.110
It doesn't exist.

00:14:45.110 --> 00:14:47.350
You better go to the government.

00:14:47.350 --> 00:14:52.400
So I went to the government,
again, a long story.

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:55.310
Finally, we persuaded the
[INAUDIBLE] ultimately,

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:59.920
and we became a bank in 1983.

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:01.660
We were so delighted
that finally we

00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:05.910
got ourselves a bank
for the poor people.

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:08.420
So what is the difference
between the bank

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:12.020
for the poor people
and banks that we know,

00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:14.210
the conventional banks?

00:15:14.210 --> 00:15:18.740
The basic difference is
the conventional banks

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:24.410
are built around the principle
that the more you have,

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:27.690
the more you can get.

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:33.840
Which meant if you have little
or nothing, you get nothing.

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:40.520
And that's why 2/3 of the world
population don't get anything.

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:46.460
So we reversed that principle
in designing our bank.

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:50.530
Our principle is
the less you have,

00:15:50.530 --> 00:15:53.820
the higher priority you get.

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:58.430
If you have nothing, you
get the highest priority.

00:15:58.430 --> 00:16:02.470
And we think that's what
the banking should be.

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:04.228
After all, people who
don't have anything,

00:16:04.228 --> 00:16:06.520
they should be the ones who
should be getting something

00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:07.270
to get it started.

00:16:09.730 --> 00:16:13.450
And then people say it
may work in Bangladesh,

00:16:13.450 --> 00:16:15.070
but it will never
work anywhere else.

00:16:15.070 --> 00:16:19.030
Bangladesh is a funny country.

00:16:19.030 --> 00:16:22.270
All kinds of funny
things happen there.

00:16:22.270 --> 00:16:25.440
So maybe this is
another funny thing.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:29.740
Well, luckily, many became
interested in our experience.

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:32.380
They want to do it
in their countries.

00:16:32.380 --> 00:16:35.590
And gradually, it came out
in Malaysia, in Philippines,

00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:37.030
in Indonesia.

00:16:37.030 --> 00:16:42.120
We are very happy that it's
working in other countries.

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:44.550
Today, after all these
years, Grameen Bank

00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:47.650
works all over Bangladesh.

00:16:47.650 --> 00:16:55.430
We have over 5 million
borrowers right now, 96% women.

00:16:55.430 --> 00:17:02.520
We lend out over a half a
billion a year in tiny loans,

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:07.670
averaging about $120 right now.

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:11.660
The money that we lend out
comes from the deposits.

00:17:11.660 --> 00:17:13.130
It's not a bank.

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:15.800
Some people, there's donors
are giving the money,

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:17.119
and they're distributing it.

00:17:17.119 --> 00:17:18.450
No, it's not like that.

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:21.880
It's just like a bank
is supposed to be--

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:23.329
taking deposits
and lending money.

00:17:23.329 --> 00:17:24.079
That's what we do.

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:32.750
On top of it, what we have
done, unlike the banks that

00:17:32.750 --> 00:17:33.980
were familiar with--

00:17:33.980 --> 00:17:39.430
the conventional banks--
we focus on women.

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:42.850
96% of our borrowers are women.

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:45.280
No collateral needed.

00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:48.040
No legal instruments are needed.

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:50.272
So there is no legal
instrument between the lender

00:17:50.272 --> 00:17:50.980
and the borrower.

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:53.800
You don't have to sign,
and notarize, and stamp,

00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:56.920
and all kinds of legal papers.

00:17:56.920 --> 00:17:59.280
We don't have any legal papers.

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:00.450
How does it work?

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:02.370
You five half a billion
dollars and no papers?

00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:08.340
And bankers tell
me, can you sleep?

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:12.690
I said I have very
peaceful sleep.

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:14.920
I have no problem.

00:18:14.920 --> 00:18:18.330
Said without papers,
you gave all this money?

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:21.180
What if it doesn't come back?

00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:23.940
I said I don't worry about it
because ever since we created,

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:25.710
money always comes back.

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:28.710
And it still does, 99%.

00:18:28.710 --> 00:18:31.590
And the 1% is not money lost.

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:35.190
It's simply not paid within
the time limit, that's all.

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:38.410
It gets paid back, too,
after the time limit.

00:18:38.410 --> 00:18:41.610
So it's a very happy situation.

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:47.490
We have savings, people
put money into the bank.

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:49.620
Right from the beginning,
we concentrated

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:52.620
on the children of the
families of Grameen Bank.

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:55.530
These are poor families.

00:18:55.530 --> 00:19:00.150
They don't have any education,
illiterate families.

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:03.095
So we wanted to make sure our
children of Grameen families

00:19:03.095 --> 00:19:05.970
are going to school.

00:19:05.970 --> 00:19:08.140
And very soon, we
succeeded in doing that.

00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:11.680
100% of the children of
Grameen families are in school.

00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:15.610
Today, many of them are
in higher education,

00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:18.250
going to colleges and
universities, medical schools,

00:19:18.250 --> 00:19:19.660
engineering schools.

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:23.050
And Grameen Bank provides
them the student loans

00:19:23.050 --> 00:19:27.280
so that they don't have to
worry about money for financing

00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:30.140
their education.

00:19:30.140 --> 00:19:37.370
There's kind of a
discussion of criticism

00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:41.850
that microcredit
is a great idea,

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:43.860
and it's wonderful
for the poor people.

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:46.620
But it works only
for the top layer

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:55.560
of the poor people who have some
entrepreneurship, some skill.

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:57.210
It doesn't work for
the middle level

00:19:57.210 --> 00:20:01.620
of the poor people, bottom
level of the poor people.

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:02.730
They need charity.

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:04.560
They need handouts.

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:07.710
Because they don't have
[INAUDIBLE] ability.

00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:10.280
They don't have any skill.

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:15.710
We scream, that's not true.

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:19.340
Because in Bangladesh,
when we begin,

00:20:19.340 --> 00:20:23.940
our loans are less than
$1, $27 to 42 people,

00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:26.270
that's our starting point.

00:20:26.270 --> 00:20:28.630
So these are not the top
layer the poor people

00:20:28.630 --> 00:20:29.650
who receive this money.

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:35.740
And then we
concentrated on women.

00:20:35.740 --> 00:20:39.530
These are not the Harvard
case studies or MIT case

00:20:39.530 --> 00:20:42.670
studies of entrepreneurship.

00:20:42.670 --> 00:20:44.910
The women coming and
pleading with us, please

00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:47.368
don't give me the money, I
don't know anything about money.

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:52.380
Imagine that.

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:54.360
You go to the bank,
bank said well

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:56.810
you justify why we
should give you a bank.

00:20:56.810 --> 00:20:58.860
Here we are going to the
people to give money,

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:02.322
they said, please
don't give us money.

00:21:02.322 --> 00:21:03.780
So that's the kind
of bank we done.

00:21:06.470 --> 00:21:10.020
And they became
so desperate, they

00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:12.580
were arguing that please
give it to my husband.

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:13.540
He understands money.

00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:15.880
I don't understand money.

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:18.740
Some said I never
touched money in my life.

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:25.180
So now you tell me, the
entrepreneurship skill

00:21:25.180 --> 00:21:31.200
and so on, we went there
deliberately addressing them,

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:32.120
and brought them in.

00:21:32.120 --> 00:21:35.025
But still people
go around saying,

00:21:35.025 --> 00:21:36.900
well, is it a great
idea, but it doesn't work

00:21:36.900 --> 00:21:39.930
for the middle and the lower.

00:21:39.930 --> 00:21:41.890
So recently what we
have done is we have

00:21:41.890 --> 00:21:45.200
got fed up with this argument.

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:48.740
We said, only way we can
stop this argument by kind

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:52.610
of dramatizing the whole thing.

00:21:52.610 --> 00:21:54.425
We said, let's give
loans to the beggars.

00:21:58.390 --> 00:22:01.000
You can't be poorer
than beggars.

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:03.550
That's the last stage.

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:06.700
Because one doesn't
have anything else,

00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:12.980
so she goes on the street,
beg from people for finding

00:22:12.980 --> 00:22:14.270
some food to survive.

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:20.570
So my colleagues ask,
what do we do with them?

00:22:20.570 --> 00:22:23.500
We said, OK, let's
go and talk to them.

00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:25.980
One thing we should
do, find out when she

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:28.610
became a beggar, at what point.

00:22:28.610 --> 00:22:33.010
Because this is a very important
point, making a transition

00:22:33.010 --> 00:22:38.090
from a normal human life to
the position of a beggar.

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:40.790
It's not easy.

00:22:40.790 --> 00:22:42.540
So that we understand
her better.

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:44.060
And after we have
learned all this,

00:22:44.060 --> 00:22:47.330
and then we sympathize
and say, OK,

00:22:47.330 --> 00:22:51.640
as you go from house
to house begging,

00:22:51.640 --> 00:22:55.380
would you like to carry
some merchandise with you,

00:22:55.380 --> 00:23:02.570
some candies, some cookies,
some toys for the kids,

00:23:02.570 --> 00:23:05.640
and give people option?

00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:10.520
Well, they will give you some
food or buy something from you.

00:23:10.520 --> 00:23:13.010
If you're selling
thing picks up,

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:15.200
you think you are
making enough, you

00:23:15.200 --> 00:23:19.960
think whether you should switch
to selling rather than begging.

00:23:19.960 --> 00:23:22.340
It's up to you.

00:23:22.340 --> 00:23:24.650
And they started
liking this idea.

00:23:24.650 --> 00:23:27.880
And in the beginning, we
thought we may have 3,000, 4,000

00:23:27.880 --> 00:23:30.300
beggars in the program.

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:36.750
Soon, we came up with 50,000
beggars in the program.

00:23:36.750 --> 00:23:40.290
And excitement of
turning themselves

00:23:40.290 --> 00:23:42.610
into business
person, you wouldn't

00:23:42.610 --> 00:23:43.860
believe what it means to them.

00:23:46.790 --> 00:23:49.710
And we tell them, look,
Grameen Bank rules

00:23:49.710 --> 00:23:51.370
do not apply for you.

00:23:51.370 --> 00:23:52.950
So don't worry
about all our rules.

00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:55.550
Forget it.

00:23:55.550 --> 00:23:58.370
Only thing, this is a loan.

00:23:58.370 --> 00:24:02.800
You'll have to pay
back as you can.

00:24:02.800 --> 00:24:05.290
No interest-- it's
interest-free loan.

00:24:05.290 --> 00:24:08.030
So don't worry about money
growing into big money.

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:09.190
So take it.

00:24:09.190 --> 00:24:13.650
And never pay us with
your begging income.

00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:14.710
That's not our money.

00:24:14.710 --> 00:24:16.770
This is your money.

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:21.080
All we want, if you make
money by selling things,

00:24:21.080 --> 00:24:22.760
then you can pay us back.

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:27.440
If you pay back the loan, you'll
be ready for another loan.

00:24:27.440 --> 00:24:30.550
People understood
it very clearly.

00:24:30.550 --> 00:24:36.350
A typical loan
for beggars is $9.

00:24:36.350 --> 00:24:40.150
Imagine, with $9, people
thinking about a business idea

00:24:40.150 --> 00:24:45.050
and how to bring herself
out of the begging.

00:24:45.050 --> 00:24:47.570
Out of this-- now that
it's ongoing program,

00:24:47.570 --> 00:24:53.760
we have about 1,000 beggars who
are no longer beggars anymore.

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:54.860
They are business people.

00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:58.610
We said our success would be
if we can see, within a year,

00:24:58.610 --> 00:25:04.310
within six months, 3,000,
5,000 have come out of begging.

00:25:04.310 --> 00:25:05.730
I said, this is--

00:25:05.730 --> 00:25:11.510
and what a message for all of us
that people can pull themselves

00:25:11.510 --> 00:25:16.560
out of all kinds of situations,
given an opportunity.

00:25:16.560 --> 00:25:21.780
So this idea of giving
loans and building up

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:25.410
this kind of financing
institution caught up

00:25:25.410 --> 00:25:31.050
globally, and in many
countries is being used.

00:25:31.050 --> 00:25:35.180
This also led us to think
about other things, information

00:25:35.180 --> 00:25:37.090
technology for example.

00:25:37.090 --> 00:25:39.320
We thought not only
microcredit is important,

00:25:39.320 --> 00:25:42.770
microfinance is important.

00:25:42.770 --> 00:25:46.190
Information technology is also
important for poor people.

00:25:46.190 --> 00:25:50.780
We got an opportunity, and
skipping the whole story,

00:25:50.780 --> 00:25:54.890
we created a mobile phone
company called Grameen Phone.

00:25:54.890 --> 00:25:57.260
Everybody-- that's a crazy idea.

00:25:57.260 --> 00:25:59.150
You are lending
money to poor people.

00:25:59.150 --> 00:26:02.510
Why do you want a
cell phone company?

00:26:02.510 --> 00:26:04.010
We said, we want
to bring cell phone

00:26:04.010 --> 00:26:07.040
in the villages of
Bangladesh, and give loans

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:11.780
to the poor women so that she
can buy herself a cell phone

00:26:11.780 --> 00:26:15.800
and start selling the service
of a cell phone in the village

00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:18.777
and make money.

00:26:18.777 --> 00:26:20.860
Who is going to use the
cell phone in the village?

00:26:20.860 --> 00:26:24.790
I said, whoever wants to
make a call to anybody.

00:26:24.790 --> 00:26:29.390
Everyone thought, this,
it will never work.

00:26:29.390 --> 00:26:32.550
But it became a
rolling business.

00:26:32.550 --> 00:26:36.660
Women took loans, started
selling service in the village.

00:26:36.660 --> 00:26:41.410
And many villages in
Bangladesh have no electricity.

00:26:41.410 --> 00:26:44.020
70% of the population
of Bangladesh

00:26:44.020 --> 00:26:46.900
do not have access
to electricity.

00:26:46.900 --> 00:26:50.010
So how do you bring cell
phones in the village

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:51.420
where there is no electricity?

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:54.470
And most of the villages
don't have electricity.

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:56.740
We promptly came
up with an idea.

00:26:56.740 --> 00:26:59.870
Solar panel, that's it.

00:26:59.870 --> 00:27:02.460
So we started plugging
into solar panels.

00:27:02.460 --> 00:27:06.530
So now everywhere
[? it helps, ?] mobile phones.

00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:09.350
There are 170,000
telephone ladies

00:27:09.350 --> 00:27:15.430
in Bangladesh selling service
of telephone who never

00:27:15.430 --> 00:27:17.880
saw telephone in their
life, never heard that there

00:27:17.880 --> 00:27:21.070
is a gadget called telephone.

00:27:21.070 --> 00:27:22.690
People used to tell
us, well, you're

00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:25.930
giving money to
the women, OK, all

00:27:25.930 --> 00:27:30.690
she can do all her life is
to raise chicken, or make

00:27:30.690 --> 00:27:35.680
basket, or raise a cow.

00:27:35.680 --> 00:27:39.380
Because she cannot come out
of the traditional technology,

00:27:39.380 --> 00:27:39.880
ever.

00:27:43.610 --> 00:27:45.310
So when we wanted
to give telephones,

00:27:45.310 --> 00:27:47.860
they said, no way,
she will be scared.

00:27:50.460 --> 00:27:52.630
And I was always arguing
that don't underestimate

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:56.280
the talent of human beings.

00:27:56.280 --> 00:27:58.740
They are very creative people.

00:27:58.740 --> 00:28:02.880
There is no difference between
the poor women and anybody

00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:05.470
else.

00:28:05.470 --> 00:28:08.670
So that is only
something in an idea.

00:28:08.670 --> 00:28:12.910
But it's wonderful
to see that people

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:17.310
reacted exactly the same way.

00:28:17.310 --> 00:28:21.140
Now today you go and talk to any
telephone lady in Bangladesh,

00:28:21.140 --> 00:28:22.750
you can make a call from here.

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:24.950
[AUDIENCE CHUCKLING]

00:28:24.950 --> 00:28:28.310
They talk in the
business language.

00:28:28.310 --> 00:28:32.570
She talks as if she was born
with a telephone in her hand.

00:28:32.570 --> 00:28:36.700
She knows everything, ins
and outs of a telephone.

00:28:36.700 --> 00:28:40.830
What a confident person she is.

00:28:40.830 --> 00:28:46.990
People ask me, what is this
that you see as poverty?

00:28:50.260 --> 00:28:53.490
How do you define poverty?

00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:57.810
I said, I don't know
how to define poverty.

00:28:57.810 --> 00:29:04.580
But to me it's something that
I see as a kind of image which

00:29:04.580 --> 00:29:07.880
comes close to my mind.

00:29:07.880 --> 00:29:09.180
It's a bonsai tree.

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:17.510
That bonsai, little tree, I say
poor people are bonsai people.

00:29:22.120 --> 00:29:26.890
You choose the tallest
tree in the forest.

00:29:26.890 --> 00:29:29.700
It's a beautiful tall
tree in the forest.

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:32.460
And pick up the best
seed for that tree.

00:29:34.970 --> 00:29:39.180
And plant it in a flower pot.

00:29:39.180 --> 00:29:40.660
What do you get?

00:29:40.660 --> 00:29:42.277
Tiny little tree.

00:29:42.277 --> 00:29:43.360
It doesn't grow very much.

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:48.620
It looks exactly like the
tree you saw in the forest.

00:29:48.620 --> 00:29:50.420
It's a small replica
of that tree.

00:29:54.220 --> 00:29:56.800
Is there something wrong
with the seed of the tree?

00:29:56.800 --> 00:30:01.160
No, we picked the best seed.

00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:03.620
Then why is it so small?

00:30:03.620 --> 00:30:06.925
Because we plant it
in a small flower pot.

00:30:06.925 --> 00:30:13.420
We didn't give the tree
space enough to grow.

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:21.140
So it's not the fault of the
seed, it's the space denied.

00:30:21.140 --> 00:30:25.930
Poor people are
just like that tree.

00:30:25.930 --> 00:30:28.970
They're as good human
being as anybody else.

00:30:28.970 --> 00:30:32.600
Simply they are not
given the space.

00:30:32.600 --> 00:30:35.690
So the poverty is not
created by them, it's

00:30:35.690 --> 00:30:39.800
created by the institutions
we built. Banking institution

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:41.870
is one.

00:30:41.870 --> 00:30:44.780
Policies that we pursued,
even the concepts

00:30:44.780 --> 00:30:49.260
that we design and we
discuss in our classrooms,

00:30:49.260 --> 00:30:55.030
ultimately impacts on the
society that we create.

00:30:55.030 --> 00:30:58.540
So if you want to get the
poverty out of the world,

00:30:58.540 --> 00:31:02.560
you have to redesign
those concepts,

00:31:02.560 --> 00:31:07.950
redesign those
theoretical structures.

00:31:07.950 --> 00:31:11.040
If you fix those things,
nobody will be poor person.

00:31:14.230 --> 00:31:19.510
Because their ability to
take care of themselves

00:31:19.510 --> 00:31:21.730
is inside of them.

00:31:21.730 --> 00:31:27.050
All human beings have
unlimited potentials.

00:31:27.050 --> 00:31:32.320
Most of us in the world
scratch only the surface,

00:31:32.320 --> 00:31:38.740
and the poor people never
even scratch the surface.

00:31:38.740 --> 00:31:43.330
So the gift each human
being has inside of them,

00:31:43.330 --> 00:31:49.560
among the poor people, that
give remains unwrapped.

00:31:49.560 --> 00:31:50.560
It's been packed.

00:31:53.120 --> 00:31:55.460
It never gets opened.

00:31:55.460 --> 00:31:58.790
So when we talk about
microcredit or all those things

00:31:58.790 --> 00:32:02.150
that I'm talking about,
this gives an opportunity

00:32:02.150 --> 00:32:06.990
to make a little bit of
openings, find out who I am.

00:32:06.990 --> 00:32:08.265
And that's the one--

00:32:08.265 --> 00:32:13.450
that's the important thing,
to see what is inside of me

00:32:13.450 --> 00:32:15.685
and how much of it
I can bring out.

00:32:15.685 --> 00:32:17.060
And if you can
allow that, if you

00:32:17.060 --> 00:32:19.950
can create that kind
of society, there's

00:32:19.950 --> 00:32:23.460
no reason why anybody
should languish

00:32:23.460 --> 00:32:27.780
in that misery and suffering.

00:32:27.780 --> 00:32:29.270
And one way to do it--

00:32:29.270 --> 00:32:31.400
I'll quickly stop at that--

00:32:31.400 --> 00:32:35.450
is to redesign the
concept of business.

00:32:35.450 --> 00:32:41.170
The business today is understood
everywhere, in our textbooks,

00:32:41.170 --> 00:32:44.020
in our theory, in
our real world.

00:32:44.020 --> 00:32:48.820
Business mean business
to make money.

00:32:48.820 --> 00:32:53.720
I said, interpretation of
human beings in that way

00:32:53.720 --> 00:32:55.350
is a very limited way.

00:32:55.350 --> 00:32:59.830
Human beings are much more--

00:32:59.830 --> 00:33:02.530
it has much more variety
inside of a human being

00:33:02.530 --> 00:33:04.120
than just that.

00:33:04.120 --> 00:33:07.330
Human beings cross
the whole range

00:33:07.330 --> 00:33:12.800
of feelings, whole range of
dimensions inside of them.

00:33:12.800 --> 00:33:17.570
So I said, if we could
include, in a idea of business,

00:33:17.570 --> 00:33:20.290
as two kinds of business.

00:33:20.290 --> 00:33:23.290
Business to make
money is one, business

00:33:23.290 --> 00:33:27.480
to do good to other
people is another one.

00:33:27.480 --> 00:33:30.380
We can run business
to do good to people.

00:33:30.380 --> 00:33:33.350
These would be
businesses which are not

00:33:33.350 --> 00:33:36.860
concerned to make profit,
but to reach out to people,

00:33:36.860 --> 00:33:39.680
solve social problems
in a business way.

00:33:39.680 --> 00:33:43.590
These are non-loss companies.

00:33:43.590 --> 00:33:44.810
It doesn't lose money.

00:33:44.810 --> 00:33:46.680
It's a break-even company.

00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:47.610
Could be done.

00:33:47.610 --> 00:33:50.850
And we can address most of the
social problems in a business

00:33:50.850 --> 00:33:51.720
way.

00:33:51.720 --> 00:33:55.080
If we can put them in a
business way, social business,

00:33:55.080 --> 00:33:57.060
this we can call
social business--

00:33:57.060 --> 00:34:01.410
if we can make social business
enterprises, a lot of problems

00:34:01.410 --> 00:34:03.660
can be addressed that way.

00:34:03.660 --> 00:34:06.700
Problem of poverty-- like
microcredit, for example,

00:34:06.700 --> 00:34:08.460
can be addressed that way--

00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:13.620
problem of children
suffering, child labor,

00:34:13.620 --> 00:34:20.780
livelihood creation, crimes,
drinking water, sanitation,

00:34:20.780 --> 00:34:26.900
health care, and social
business enterprises.

00:34:26.900 --> 00:34:29.239
We need social
business entrepreneurs.

00:34:29.239 --> 00:34:31.352
People say ah ha,
I want to do this.

00:34:31.352 --> 00:34:32.810
This is a challenge
I want to take.

00:34:32.810 --> 00:34:36.800
I can design things and
I can run a business,

00:34:36.800 --> 00:34:39.980
not for me to make money,
but to reach out and solve

00:34:39.980 --> 00:34:40.770
the problem.

00:34:40.770 --> 00:34:42.270
So this is a challenge.

00:34:42.270 --> 00:34:45.530
This is an idea
what I think time

00:34:45.530 --> 00:34:48.830
has come to address
lots of problems

00:34:48.830 --> 00:34:50.679
that we see around ourselves.

00:34:50.679 --> 00:34:51.679
And thank you very much.

00:34:51.679 --> 00:34:52.670
I'll stop here.

00:34:52.670 --> 00:34:54.658
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:54.658 --> 00:34:56.149
Thank you.

00:34:56.149 --> 00:34:58.137
Thank you.

00:34:58.137 --> 00:35:01.119
Thank you.

00:35:01.119 --> 00:35:02.113
Thank you.

00:35:02.113 --> 00:35:04.101
[APPLAUSE CONTINUES]

00:35:04.101 --> 00:35:06.089
Thank you.

00:35:06.089 --> 00:35:07.083
Thank you very much.

00:35:11.059 --> 00:35:12.053
Thank you.

00:35:23.422 --> 00:35:24.880
MODERATOR: We don't
have much time.

00:35:24.880 --> 00:35:29.480
Unfortunately Dr. Yunus has
to head back to New York.

00:35:29.480 --> 00:35:31.060
It's a busy week for him.

00:35:31.060 --> 00:35:33.490
So we have microphones
somewhere here.

00:35:33.490 --> 00:35:34.790
Yes, here and there.

00:35:34.790 --> 00:35:36.317
And so if you would
please come up.

00:35:36.317 --> 00:35:37.900
And I'm sorry, it's
the only way we're

00:35:37.900 --> 00:35:40.430
going to be able to hear you.

00:35:40.430 --> 00:35:43.820
And if you'll just line up, and
we'll try to get as many of you

00:35:43.820 --> 00:35:44.320
in.

00:35:44.320 --> 00:35:46.580
Be as brief and
concise as possible.

00:35:46.580 --> 00:35:49.390
Thank you.

00:35:49.390 --> 00:35:51.828
Please go ahead.

00:35:51.828 --> 00:35:53.370
AUDIENCE: It's a
very inspiring thing

00:35:53.370 --> 00:35:54.537
you do for all those people.

00:35:54.537 --> 00:35:56.140
I had a practical
question, which

00:35:56.140 --> 00:36:00.340
is how specifically do you guys
actually run the operation?

00:36:00.340 --> 00:36:03.160
How do you collect, how do you
determine how much interest you

00:36:03.160 --> 00:36:07.090
charge and how much interest
people get in their deposits?

00:36:07.090 --> 00:36:09.520
YUNUS: I will have a one
full-blown lecture for that.

00:36:12.790 --> 00:36:14.950
We have branches.

00:36:14.950 --> 00:36:18.880
We have over 1,600 branches
in the country in Bangladesh.

00:36:18.880 --> 00:36:23.050
Each staff is responsible for
a certain number of borrowers,

00:36:23.050 --> 00:36:27.190
to take care of their
needs and so on.

00:36:27.190 --> 00:36:30.280
All Grameen Bank
activities are organized

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:33.520
around building
five-member groups.

00:36:33.520 --> 00:36:37.330
Five people form a kind
of a business circle.

00:36:37.330 --> 00:36:39.970
And several of those
groups together

00:36:39.970 --> 00:36:42.710
create what we call a center.

00:36:42.710 --> 00:36:47.630
The center meets each week where
the business is transacted.

00:36:47.630 --> 00:36:50.795
Our basic principle is people
should not come to the bank,

00:36:50.795 --> 00:36:52.520
the bank should
go to the people.

00:36:52.520 --> 00:36:54.170
So we always go to the people.

00:36:54.170 --> 00:36:57.800
Every week, we meet all
our five million borrowers

00:36:57.800 --> 00:37:00.700
at their doorstep, and do the
business at their doorstep.

00:37:00.700 --> 00:37:04.680
And all our loans are paid
back in weekly installments.

00:37:04.680 --> 00:37:09.650
So this is basically
the operational details.

00:37:09.650 --> 00:37:11.550
AUDIENCE: And what's
the average interest

00:37:11.550 --> 00:37:12.508
rate you charge people?

00:37:12.508 --> 00:37:15.480
YUNUS: Interest rate,
we try to be as close as

00:37:15.480 --> 00:37:17.940
possible to the
market interest rate.

00:37:17.940 --> 00:37:22.920
Market interest rate today
in Bangladesh is about 14%.

00:37:22.920 --> 00:37:25.510
We have four interest
rates in our work.

00:37:25.510 --> 00:37:30.386
One is 20%, which is 6%
above the market rate.

00:37:30.386 --> 00:37:32.400
And the next one is
the housing loan.

00:37:32.400 --> 00:37:37.410
Housing loan is 8%, which is
way below the market rate.

00:37:37.410 --> 00:37:41.550
And next one is 5% for the
student loan, which is again

00:37:41.550 --> 00:37:43.510
way below the market rate.

00:37:43.510 --> 00:37:47.180
And for beggars, it's 0%.

00:37:47.180 --> 00:37:51.900
So together we are
close to market rate.

00:37:51.900 --> 00:37:54.720
And we also a deposit collector.

00:37:54.720 --> 00:37:57.090
So on our deposits,
we pay 8 and 1/2%

00:37:57.090 --> 00:37:59.580
interest on deposits, minimum.

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:02.190
And it goes all the way to 12%.

00:38:02.190 --> 00:38:09.280
So we pay 8 and 1/2% to 12%, and
we charge 20%, 8%, 5%, and 0%.

00:38:09.280 --> 00:38:10.020
That's it.

00:38:10.020 --> 00:38:10.230
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:38:10.230 --> 00:38:10.938
YUNUS: Thank you.

00:38:10.938 --> 00:38:11.770
MODERATOR: Please.

00:38:11.770 --> 00:38:12.630
AUDIENCE: Hello.

00:38:12.630 --> 00:38:16.692
Thank you, Dr. Yunus, for your
comments and for all your work.

00:38:16.692 --> 00:38:17.400
My name is Raven.

00:38:17.400 --> 00:38:18.970
I'm from the Fletcher School.

00:38:18.970 --> 00:38:22.980
And with all the
kind of enthusiasm

00:38:22.980 --> 00:38:24.930
surrounding
microfinance right now,

00:38:24.930 --> 00:38:27.120
I've heard it said
as a word of caution

00:38:27.120 --> 00:38:30.990
that microcredit especially,
offered first to the poor,

00:38:30.990 --> 00:38:34.888
can really cause harm to
those who are in need of it.

00:38:34.888 --> 00:38:36.930
And I'm curious, just as
your comments and advice

00:38:36.930 --> 00:38:39.600
to those enthusiastic
about microfinance, what

00:38:39.600 --> 00:38:41.760
are you doing and
what do you recommend

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:45.240
should be done in the way of
offering other interventions

00:38:45.240 --> 00:38:47.790
to ensure that this
capital provided to them

00:38:47.790 --> 00:38:49.610
doesn't in fact indebt them.

00:38:49.610 --> 00:38:50.840
Thank you.

00:38:50.840 --> 00:38:52.860
YUNUS: Yeah.

00:38:52.860 --> 00:38:57.560
One thing to be careful when
you give loans to people,

00:38:57.560 --> 00:39:00.500
we try to make sure
that this money is used

00:39:00.500 --> 00:39:02.570
for income-generating purposes.

00:39:02.570 --> 00:39:05.450
Money should create money.

00:39:05.450 --> 00:39:08.750
Once you consume it
up, take the money,

00:39:08.750 --> 00:39:12.470
and then you create yourself
the obligations, and the debt

00:39:12.470 --> 00:39:14.640
burden, and all kinds of things.

00:39:14.640 --> 00:39:17.210
So that's why we created all
of those groups and so on.

00:39:17.210 --> 00:39:20.180
You can mutually oversee
each other's activities

00:39:20.180 --> 00:39:23.210
so that you don't use the
money in the wrong way.

00:39:23.210 --> 00:39:27.260
Because we give loans for
income-generating activity.

00:39:27.260 --> 00:39:29.450
So you have to
demonstrate, you have

00:39:29.450 --> 00:39:32.270
to explain to your
own group members

00:39:32.270 --> 00:39:34.070
what you are going
to do with the money,

00:39:34.070 --> 00:39:37.100
how this money will
bring money for you.

00:39:37.100 --> 00:39:40.340
Bank doesn't approve the loan.

00:39:40.340 --> 00:39:43.710
Basic approval is done
by her own friends.

00:39:43.710 --> 00:39:45.920
So she has to explain
everything what

00:39:45.920 --> 00:39:48.600
she is going to do so that
they can go and check it out.

00:39:48.600 --> 00:39:54.140
So this is one way we see to
protect them from using up

00:39:54.140 --> 00:39:55.690
the money in the wrong way.

00:39:55.690 --> 00:39:58.070
AUDIENCE: Are there
any other activities

00:39:58.070 --> 00:40:02.810
such as capacity-building,
or market value chain

00:40:02.810 --> 00:40:04.760
improvements, or
productivity-enhancement

00:40:04.760 --> 00:40:07.470
activities that the bank
does along with the lending?

00:40:07.470 --> 00:40:10.770
YUNUS: We don't have any
program of training as such,

00:40:10.770 --> 00:40:14.640
but we try to connect
them with each other.

00:40:14.640 --> 00:40:16.880
If you are doing something,
and somebody else

00:40:16.880 --> 00:40:19.400
is doing the similar
thing in the next center,

00:40:19.400 --> 00:40:20.940
we say, ah, she
is doing this, why

00:40:20.940 --> 00:40:23.600
don't you get together
and see what do you do.

00:40:23.600 --> 00:40:26.420
They learn from each
other much faster.

00:40:26.420 --> 00:40:31.020
And one thing comes because
of some kind of jealousy

00:40:31.020 --> 00:40:34.070
that she is doing so good
and I'm not doing it.

00:40:34.070 --> 00:40:36.690
So she would like
to be equal to her.

00:40:36.690 --> 00:40:38.120
So this is also good.

00:40:38.120 --> 00:40:44.000
This is a creative jealousy that
she tries to get even with her

00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:47.370
and prove that she can
perform better than her.

00:40:47.370 --> 00:40:51.140
It's a neighborly jealousy.

00:40:51.140 --> 00:40:52.760
That works pretty well.

00:40:52.760 --> 00:40:55.700
And we encourage-- we always
give examples of what others

00:40:55.700 --> 00:40:58.042
are doing, so that she feels--

00:40:58.042 --> 00:40:58.886
[AUDIENCE CHUCKLING]

00:40:58.886 --> 00:41:02.110
She feels, ah ha,
how can she do that?

00:41:02.110 --> 00:41:02.900
But thank you.

00:41:02.900 --> 00:41:03.470
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:41:03.470 --> 00:41:04.022
YUNUS: Yeah.

00:41:04.022 --> 00:41:05.230
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:05.230 --> 00:41:07.830
MODERATOR: Please,
one question each.

00:41:07.830 --> 00:41:08.540
No follow-up.

00:41:08.540 --> 00:41:10.510
I'm sorry-- just to
allow everyone a chance.

00:41:10.510 --> 00:41:12.510
AUDIENCE: Hey, Dr. Yunus,
Juan Garcia from ICIC.

00:41:12.510 --> 00:41:14.660
Thank you for coming
and speaking to us.

00:41:14.660 --> 00:41:18.120
I read your book, felt very
empowered, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:41:18.120 --> 00:41:21.680
My question is, in your
book, you touch a little

00:41:21.680 --> 00:41:24.770
bit about the example of
microcredit financing here

00:41:24.770 --> 00:41:26.240
in the US.

00:41:26.240 --> 00:41:31.230
And it seems it was not as
successful as we had expected.

00:41:31.230 --> 00:41:33.920
So in the wake of Katrina,
and with all the people

00:41:33.920 --> 00:41:37.100
left behind with nothing,
how can an example like yours

00:41:37.100 --> 00:41:38.930
become relevant?

00:41:38.930 --> 00:41:40.040
YUNUS: Very much.

00:41:40.040 --> 00:41:41.480
I just wrote an op ed--

00:41:41.480 --> 00:41:44.450
I don't know if anybody
published it yet--

00:41:44.450 --> 00:41:45.380
mentioning that.

00:41:45.380 --> 00:41:49.940
Because Bangladesh has
lots of experience of going

00:41:49.940 --> 00:41:51.290
through floods and disaster.

00:41:51.290 --> 00:41:52.730
This is our normal life.

00:41:52.730 --> 00:41:55.230
We face this all the time.

00:41:55.230 --> 00:41:59.370
So we had to learn
this over time.

00:41:59.370 --> 00:42:02.180
One of the things we find
very useful in a situation

00:42:02.180 --> 00:42:05.810
of disaster-- microcredit-- to
build it back again quickly,

00:42:05.810 --> 00:42:09.950
because of all the
mechanism is set

00:42:09.950 --> 00:42:13.695
to get you started all over
again, put you on your feet.

00:42:13.695 --> 00:42:15.070
Here, [INAUDIBLE]
this would been

00:42:15.070 --> 00:42:18.120
an occasion in New Orleans.

00:42:18.120 --> 00:42:21.830
Tried two things-- one is
microcredit, crediting channels

00:42:21.830 --> 00:42:24.422
so that people can go
back and start their life

00:42:24.422 --> 00:42:25.880
all over again,
build their houses,

00:42:25.880 --> 00:42:27.705
build their
livelihood and so on.

00:42:27.705 --> 00:42:29.330
Another one, this is
a good opportunity

00:42:29.330 --> 00:42:33.050
also for building up social
business enterprises,

00:42:33.050 --> 00:42:35.720
social entrepreneurs coming--
social business entrepreneurs

00:42:35.720 --> 00:42:38.150
coming and designing
things in a business way

00:42:38.150 --> 00:42:40.160
to address these issues.

00:42:40.160 --> 00:42:44.360
Money going as a
handout, as a giveaway

00:42:44.360 --> 00:42:48.320
sometimes don't achieve the
purpose for which you do.

00:42:48.320 --> 00:42:50.630
But if you are doing it
in a business format,

00:42:50.630 --> 00:42:53.250
it works much faster
and much efficiently.

00:42:53.250 --> 00:42:58.485
So this is one occasion I would
think would help enormously.

00:42:58.485 --> 00:42:59.337
MODERATOR: Please.

00:42:59.337 --> 00:43:00.170
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:43:00.170 --> 00:43:03.710
Many of us are familiar with
the arsenic in the drinking

00:43:03.710 --> 00:43:05.120
water in Bangladesh.

00:43:05.120 --> 00:43:08.090
And I'm wondering if
you have taken any steps

00:43:08.090 --> 00:43:12.320
to apply your philosophy
to empowerment of women

00:43:12.320 --> 00:43:14.670
in providing safe
drinking water.

00:43:14.670 --> 00:43:15.170
YUNUS: Yeah.

00:43:15.170 --> 00:43:16.220
We try all the time.

00:43:16.220 --> 00:43:20.030
This is a terrible thing that
we have been going through.

00:43:20.030 --> 00:43:25.100
Grameen Bank has encouraged
people to have tube wells.

00:43:25.100 --> 00:43:27.710
We gave loans to
have tube wells.

00:43:27.710 --> 00:43:29.720
At that time, we had
no idea that this

00:43:29.720 --> 00:43:32.720
is going to lead to
another kind of problem.

00:43:32.720 --> 00:43:35.540
UNICEF was so enthusiastic
about tube wells.

00:43:35.540 --> 00:43:38.820
We thought, we should have tube
well for every single family

00:43:38.820 --> 00:43:43.910
in Grameen Bank, which we
generously kind of promoted.

00:43:43.910 --> 00:43:46.730
So now we feel
guilty that this is

00:43:46.730 --> 00:43:51.260
being the source of such
a terrible thing, arsenic.

00:43:51.260 --> 00:43:54.710
So quickly, what we did,
like others have done,

00:43:54.710 --> 00:43:57.440
kind of classify
those tube wells which

00:43:57.440 --> 00:44:00.860
are-- arsenic content
is so high that it's not

00:44:00.860 --> 00:44:02.690
usable for drinking purposes.

00:44:02.690 --> 00:44:06.680
Which, others are still usable
because arsenic problem doesn't

00:44:06.680 --> 00:44:07.527
exist.

00:44:07.527 --> 00:44:09.860
Once we have done that, then
we have, on a second level,

00:44:09.860 --> 00:44:14.630
where the arsenic exists,
how to clean it up.

00:44:14.630 --> 00:44:18.380
So there are
certain methods now.

00:44:18.380 --> 00:44:21.260
[? As a ?] pitcher--
three-pitcher system

00:44:21.260 --> 00:44:22.130
which we encourage.

00:44:22.130 --> 00:44:26.720
And then encouraging them to
go into rainwater harvesting.

00:44:26.720 --> 00:44:28.400
People are not
familiar in rainwater

00:44:28.400 --> 00:44:31.500
[? harvesting. ?] Bangladesh
is a country of rains.

00:44:31.500 --> 00:44:35.040
In monsoon, we get
enormous amount of rain.

00:44:35.040 --> 00:44:37.940
But people don't drink
rainwater because there's water

00:44:37.940 --> 00:44:39.120
everywhere.

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:43.280
So now we're encouraging them
to build rainwater harvesting

00:44:43.280 --> 00:44:46.700
system and purify
the surface water.

00:44:46.700 --> 00:44:49.100
But it's still the real
answer has not come.

00:44:49.100 --> 00:44:50.760
Arsenic is still
a major problem.

00:44:50.760 --> 00:44:52.100
We are still working on it.

00:44:52.100 --> 00:44:53.840
AUDIENCE: But you are
providing loans for that.

00:44:53.840 --> 00:44:54.080
YUNUS: Pardon?

00:44:54.080 --> 00:44:55.160
Yes, we provide loans.

00:44:55.160 --> 00:44:56.030
Loans, that's no problem.

00:44:56.030 --> 00:44:56.970
AUDIENCE: But that is not--

00:44:56.970 --> 00:44:58.820
YUNUS: It's the technology
that's the problem right now

00:44:58.820 --> 00:44:59.120
for us.

00:44:59.120 --> 00:45:00.710
AUDIENCE: But that is not
generating income, is it?

00:45:00.710 --> 00:45:01.210
YUNUS: No.

00:45:01.210 --> 00:45:02.150
We give housing loan.

00:45:02.150 --> 00:45:03.950
It's the same old thing.

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:05.240
We have given housing loan.

00:45:05.240 --> 00:45:06.928
We have gone to debate.

00:45:06.928 --> 00:45:08.720
Everybody said, you
don't give housing loan

00:45:08.720 --> 00:45:10.400
because it doesn't
generate income.

00:45:10.400 --> 00:45:12.250
We said, no, it
generates income.

00:45:12.250 --> 00:45:13.700
It's a very productive loan.

00:45:13.700 --> 00:45:16.520
If you can sleep peacefully,
that's very good.

00:45:16.520 --> 00:45:19.610
This gives you the
energy to do that.

00:45:19.610 --> 00:45:23.390
And for women-- because
our borrowers are women--

00:45:23.390 --> 00:45:25.670
the house is also our factory.

00:45:25.670 --> 00:45:27.360
That's where she works.

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:28.490
So if you cannot--

00:45:28.490 --> 00:45:34.160
if you leave this roof
open, she can't work.

00:45:34.160 --> 00:45:35.250
So it's very important.

00:45:35.250 --> 00:45:37.970
So what is productive,
what is non-productive

00:45:37.970 --> 00:45:39.950
is something that
you have to decide

00:45:39.950 --> 00:45:41.720
in the context of the situation.

00:45:41.720 --> 00:45:44.438
So simply say, drinking
water is not productive,

00:45:44.438 --> 00:45:45.980
it's wouldn't be a
good thing to say.

00:45:45.980 --> 00:45:46.456
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:45:46.456 --> 00:45:47.410
MODERATOR: Thank you.

00:45:47.410 --> 00:45:48.140
Please.

00:45:48.140 --> 00:45:49.630
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:45:49.630 --> 00:45:52.690
What kind of results
have you find

00:45:52.690 --> 00:46:01.240
in impact evaluation related
with poverty alleviation

00:46:01.240 --> 00:46:04.240
with the loans compared
with groups that

00:46:04.240 --> 00:46:06.890
haven't received the loans?

00:46:06.890 --> 00:46:07.890
YUNUS: Yeah.

00:46:07.890 --> 00:46:10.750
There are lots of studies
done on Grameen Bank,

00:46:10.750 --> 00:46:12.580
on many, many different issues.

00:46:12.580 --> 00:46:16.240
Grameen Bank became a very
attractive research subject

00:46:16.240 --> 00:46:19.930
for many reasons--
for women, for credit,

00:46:19.930 --> 00:46:23.330
for poverty alleviation,
for health, and so on.

00:46:23.330 --> 00:46:29.740
So in all this, they have
uniformly came to the finding

00:46:29.740 --> 00:46:33.760
that people in Grameen
Bank have moved out

00:46:33.760 --> 00:46:35.860
of poverty consistently.

00:46:35.860 --> 00:46:40.490
One World Bank study gives
a very specific number.

00:46:40.490 --> 00:46:43.270
They said, 5% of the
Grameen borrowers

00:46:43.270 --> 00:46:46.450
come out of poverty every year.

00:46:46.450 --> 00:46:48.850
And we have been
trying to make sure

00:46:48.850 --> 00:46:52.270
not only 5%, that
speed, can we make it

00:46:52.270 --> 00:46:57.970
faster so that larger and larger
percentage of Grameen borrowers

00:46:57.970 --> 00:47:01.490
get out of poverty each year,
and each month, each day,

00:47:01.490 --> 00:47:01.990
each week.

00:47:01.990 --> 00:47:04.870
Because people don't wait
until the end of the year

00:47:04.870 --> 00:47:06.400
to get out of poverty.

00:47:06.400 --> 00:47:07.840
[AUDIENCE CHUCKLING]

00:47:07.840 --> 00:47:09.220
They get out every day--

00:47:09.220 --> 00:47:11.410
one family today,
next family tomorrow,

00:47:11.410 --> 00:47:14.210
depending on where you are.

00:47:14.210 --> 00:47:16.660
So we want to see
that happening.

00:47:16.660 --> 00:47:20.830
Another study shows that
getting out of poverty

00:47:20.830 --> 00:47:25.180
starts as soon as about
five cycles of loans.

00:47:25.180 --> 00:47:28.920
But there may be families
which takes 15 cycles of loans

00:47:28.920 --> 00:47:30.440
still in poverty.

00:47:30.440 --> 00:47:34.900
So we are trying to see how to
reduce that 15 cycles to come

00:47:34.900 --> 00:47:37.300
down to 10 cycle,
and fifth cycle

00:47:37.300 --> 00:47:40.690
to come to the fourth cycle,
so that the range in between

00:47:40.690 --> 00:47:43.540
becomes shorter and shorter,
people, all kinds of people

00:47:43.540 --> 00:47:44.320
come.

00:47:44.320 --> 00:47:48.190
And we see the reason it's
prolonged for many families is

00:47:48.190 --> 00:47:50.570
sickness, disease.

00:47:50.570 --> 00:47:52.960
So the health became
another worry for us.

00:47:52.960 --> 00:47:55.780
We still can address
that health issue.

00:47:55.780 --> 00:47:57.310
And that's, we
see, the challenge

00:47:57.310 --> 00:48:00.130
for anybody who is interested
in poverty alleviation,

00:48:00.130 --> 00:48:03.460
how to address the
health care services

00:48:03.460 --> 00:48:06.970
to come to the poorest
people in an affordable way

00:48:06.970 --> 00:48:11.380
so that they can get out
of the health problems

00:48:11.380 --> 00:48:13.060
that they suffer.

00:48:13.060 --> 00:48:18.580
Poor also means poor in health.

00:48:18.580 --> 00:48:20.330
It goes together.

00:48:20.330 --> 00:48:22.870
So now you have to
address it simultaneously.

00:48:22.870 --> 00:48:25.090
We are addressing one
part, but we still

00:48:25.090 --> 00:48:26.900
couldn't get to the health part.

00:48:26.900 --> 00:48:29.750
So this is where we are now
trying to say, can we build,

00:48:29.750 --> 00:48:32.800
in a Grameen-type health
care in a virtual way

00:48:32.800 --> 00:48:35.830
within Bangladesh, to
build it up in a way

00:48:35.830 --> 00:48:39.217
that nobody is left out
of health care system.

00:48:39.217 --> 00:48:41.800
MODERATOR: We're going to have
time for a couple of questions.

00:48:41.800 --> 00:48:43.040
I'm very sorry.

00:48:43.040 --> 00:48:45.100
Please, so we'll just
go as far as we can.

00:48:45.100 --> 00:48:46.110
We have five minutes.

00:48:46.110 --> 00:48:46.970
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:48:46.970 --> 00:48:49.320
Mr. Yunus, thank you
for your inspiring talk.

00:48:49.320 --> 00:48:50.130
YUNUS: Thank you.

00:48:50.130 --> 00:48:50.960
AUDIENCE: When you
told your story,

00:48:50.960 --> 00:48:53.293
I couldn't help but think
that the banker you approached

00:48:53.293 --> 00:48:56.960
might feel like IBM does
after meeting with Bill Gates.

00:48:56.960 --> 00:48:57.940
[CHUCKLING]

00:48:57.940 --> 00:48:59.830
I have two quick questions.

00:48:59.830 --> 00:49:03.110
You can answer
whichever you like.

00:49:03.110 --> 00:49:03.610
You said--

00:49:03.610 --> 00:49:04.750
YUNUS: You give me options.

00:49:04.750 --> 00:49:06.083
AUDIENCE: Yes, options are good.

00:49:06.083 --> 00:49:08.440
Now, you said you had an
original set of rules when

00:49:08.440 --> 00:49:10.000
you made the first small leap.

00:49:10.000 --> 00:49:12.340
If you could go into
detail about those

00:49:12.340 --> 00:49:16.410
rules that you had for the
first 42 of your borrowers.

00:49:16.410 --> 00:49:18.160
And two, do you have
any advice for people

00:49:18.160 --> 00:49:21.040
who are thinking of
replicating a similar scheme

00:49:21.040 --> 00:49:22.270
for microinsurance?

00:49:22.270 --> 00:49:25.480
Because given the
disasters that take place,

00:49:25.480 --> 00:49:26.980
people often lose
their livelihoods.

00:49:26.980 --> 00:49:28.407
And we've had discussions--

00:49:28.407 --> 00:49:29.740
students-- about microinsurance.

00:49:29.740 --> 00:49:30.982
Thank you.

00:49:30.982 --> 00:49:33.190
YUNUS: Yes, microinsurance
is a very important thing.

00:49:33.190 --> 00:49:35.740
Let me take the
microinsurance part of it.

00:49:35.740 --> 00:49:38.870
We have been providing
health insurance.

00:49:38.870 --> 00:49:43.150
We have developed a health care
system, but in a limited area.

00:49:43.150 --> 00:49:45.430
We are trying it out.

00:49:45.430 --> 00:49:48.220
I'll explain a little bit more.

00:49:48.220 --> 00:49:50.770
Each Grameen Bank
family in that area

00:49:50.770 --> 00:49:53.590
where we have the micro health--

00:49:53.590 --> 00:50:00.130
insurance can join the insurance
program by paying about $3.00

00:50:00.130 --> 00:50:01.420
a year.

00:50:01.420 --> 00:50:05.320
If you put $3.00 a year, entire
family comes under the health

00:50:05.320 --> 00:50:06.970
care insurance.

00:50:06.970 --> 00:50:09.730
And for that, you have a
fully-qualified doctors

00:50:09.730 --> 00:50:13.670
in the area, and the
paramedics, and health workers,

00:50:13.670 --> 00:50:17.980
and you can buy medicine
at 25% discount,

00:50:17.980 --> 00:50:22.460
and you have pathological
lab right there, and so on.

00:50:22.460 --> 00:50:28.030
So today, in the 20 areas that
we work, we cover about 90%

00:50:28.030 --> 00:50:32.540
of our cost in expenses
that we have to incur.

00:50:32.540 --> 00:50:35.070
So still have a 10% gap.

00:50:35.070 --> 00:50:38.350
So we are trying to see if we
can come to the 10% recovery

00:50:38.350 --> 00:50:42.460
so that we can expand it
globally, all over Bangladesh.

00:50:42.460 --> 00:50:45.430
Because we cannot expand
it globally if you have

00:50:45.430 --> 00:50:48.810
to subsidize it from the
resources that we have.

00:50:48.810 --> 00:50:50.560
So this is where we
are looking for-- this

00:50:50.560 --> 00:50:51.860
is the health insurance part.

00:50:51.860 --> 00:50:54.310
Then we have the loan insurance.

00:50:54.310 --> 00:50:58.420
Loan insurance is simply
if a borrower dies

00:50:58.420 --> 00:51:00.500
and she lives behind--

00:51:00.500 --> 00:51:03.340
of course she leaves
behind the loan amount.

00:51:03.340 --> 00:51:05.950
Under this insurance,
entire loan amount

00:51:05.950 --> 00:51:09.520
is paid off by the
insurance, so the family

00:51:09.520 --> 00:51:11.120
doesn't have to worry about it.

00:51:11.120 --> 00:51:14.800
And not only it's a financial
relief for the family,

00:51:14.800 --> 00:51:17.830
it's also a great satisfaction
for the borrower herself

00:51:17.830 --> 00:51:19.150
when she is alive.

00:51:19.150 --> 00:51:21.320
Because she feels,
when she dies,

00:51:21.320 --> 00:51:24.520
she can go very clean to God.

00:51:24.520 --> 00:51:27.340
She has not left behind
any loan obligation.

00:51:27.340 --> 00:51:30.790
Because this is a part of
their religious feeling

00:51:30.790 --> 00:51:33.070
that if she leaves
behind a loan,

00:51:33.070 --> 00:51:35.050
God will be extremely
unhappy with her.

00:51:35.050 --> 00:51:39.640
Until somebody takes care of
that loan, she has to suffer.

00:51:39.640 --> 00:51:43.630
So now she feels very
happy that the insurance

00:51:43.630 --> 00:51:45.640
takes care of everything.

00:51:45.640 --> 00:51:49.470
She feels that I have not been
creating any burden on anybody

00:51:49.470 --> 00:51:50.610
in my life.

00:51:50.610 --> 00:51:53.020
When I am dead, I don't
want to leave any burden

00:51:53.020 --> 00:51:54.040
on anybody else.

00:51:54.040 --> 00:51:56.090
I can go clean.

00:51:56.090 --> 00:51:59.240
So that's loan insurance.

00:51:59.240 --> 00:52:00.550
Another one is life insurance.

00:52:00.550 --> 00:52:03.430
We also have life
insurance coverage.

00:52:03.430 --> 00:52:05.950
So this is loan
insurance, life insurance

00:52:05.950 --> 00:52:07.840
is universal within
Grameen Bank.

00:52:07.840 --> 00:52:09.190
It's already there.

00:52:09.190 --> 00:52:12.130
But the health insurance is
for just a limited experiment

00:52:12.130 --> 00:52:12.930
we are doing.

00:52:12.930 --> 00:52:14.810
We hope we will be
successful in that.

00:52:14.810 --> 00:52:15.310
Thank you.

00:52:15.310 --> 00:52:15.700
MODERATOR: Thank you.

00:52:15.700 --> 00:52:17.200
I think that's all
the time we have.

00:52:17.200 --> 00:52:17.830
I'm very sorry.

00:52:17.830 --> 00:52:18.330
Please.

00:52:21.233 --> 00:52:23.650
HOCKFIELD: Well, this has been
an extraordinary afternoon.

00:52:23.650 --> 00:52:26.380
And I want to remind you all
that none of these things

00:52:26.380 --> 00:52:28.060
happen by virtue of
a single individual.

00:52:28.060 --> 00:52:32.890
And we owe an enormous amount
of thanks to our alumnus

00:52:32.890 --> 00:52:35.410
Mohammed Jameel from
the class of 1978

00:52:35.410 --> 00:52:38.670
for making it possible for
Dr. Yunus to be with us today.

00:52:38.670 --> 00:52:40.547
MIT, we pride
ourselves by saying

00:52:40.547 --> 00:52:42.380
we're the kind of people
who solve problems.

00:52:42.380 --> 00:52:45.100
And I cannot tell you how
inspiring it is to see how one

00:52:45.100 --> 00:52:47.350
individual, through
intelligence, initiative,

00:52:47.350 --> 00:52:51.100
persistence, has made an
enormous inroad in one

00:52:51.100 --> 00:52:53.050
of the world's
greatest problems,

00:52:53.050 --> 00:52:54.520
which is global poverty.

00:52:54.520 --> 00:52:57.010
I want you to please join with
me in taking Dr. Yunus once

00:52:57.010 --> 00:52:58.300
again for an inspiring talk.

00:52:58.300 --> 00:53:01.950
[APPLAUSE]

